using hand  sanitizer is a new way of life for  everyone  hand hygiene is recognized and promoted  as the single most effective measure to  reduce the spread of coronavirus  [Music]  and this is what the world health  organization  says we should all use so we've got  absolute alcohol  hand sanitizer containing at least 60  percent alcohol  it kills the virus in 30 seconds if you  have something that has a fatty membrane  around it  the alcohol will destroy that membrane  and therefore destroy  the microorganism that you're looking  for and coronavirus does have one of  those it's called its envelope  and that's what alcohol does it kills  that envelope off and then therefore  kills the virus  but sky news has established hand  sanitizer containing  no alcohol is being sold and used across  the uk  so much of it that scotland's first  minister was filmed in a school  using hand sanitizer that doesn't meet  her own government's requirements  this factory in oxfordshire is making 75  000 liters of alcohol-free hand  sanitizer  every month entirely legally to a  european  and british standard the standard tests  it  not on hands but in a test tube this  sanitizer takes  two minutes to kill coronavirus we have  tried to follow as much of the  regulations that are available to us  the british standards that we follow we  provided the schools with the relevant  information  it has been tested to kill coronavirus  it's safe it's soft on hands it's good  for the children there's many benefits  there for them  and i think the main thing is it works  it's in  your interest on march the 3rd the  health secretary was clear  this is a national effort we need  everyone to listen to  and act on the official medical advice  at that time the official advice was use  60 alcohol the next day mr hancock said  the government was making sure the  public knows  exactly what they should be doing to  keep themselves  and others safe but on the 13th of march  the commitment to alcohol-based  sanitizer  was quietly withdrawn and now there is  no recommendation  at all a leading expert says that is  wrong  no i am concerned because when we uh we  we know we've got an effective product  in alcohol hand sanitizers 60 percent or  over 62 percent  or above i don't see why we should be  prepared to  to to settle for something which has not  been  tested under use conditions and i think  that that is a failing in response  the british standards institute told us  in a statement  if the product meets the requirements of  outcome of the  test methodology a product is deemed  to pass this enables different types of  products  to be used safely westlothian council  says its schools are no longer using  alcohol-free products in key areas  it said we use both alcohol and  non-alcohol-based products  we have adhered to the scottish  government's guidance  we asked the department for health and  social care for a response  but they have not provided one  there are now hundreds of thousands of  bottles  of alcohol-free hand sanitizer being  used  across the country the watering down of  advice from westminster  has left people unsure what they're  using  or how long it takes to kill coronavirus  on their hands gerard tub sky news

